slang: --relax-dist-precedence

`a == b dist {...}` parses as `a == (b dist {...})`, then "invalid operands to binary
expression ('bit[31:0]' and 'void')": slang gives dist the precedence of inside, which
IEEE 1800-2017 Table 11-2 lists, while the grammar (expression_or_dist ::= expression
[dist {...}]) and other tools apply the dist to the whole expression. The new
ParserOptions::distOverWholeExpression (driver option --relax-dist-precedence) makes
Parser::parseSubExpression leave a dist to the outermost level of an expression_or_dist.
Found in OpenTitan's keymgr DV (and the hmac and pattgen constraints its fixes.patch
rewrites).

--- a/include/slang/driver/Driver.h
+++ b/include/slang/driver/Driver.h
@@ -196,6 +196,9 @@ public:
         /// The maximum call stack depth of parsing before an error is issued.
         std::optional<uint32_t> maxParseDepth;
 
+        /// Parse the `dist` of an expression_or_dist over the whole expression.
+        std::optional<bool> relaxDistPrecedence;
+
         /// The maximum number of lexer errors that can be encountered before giving up.
         std::optional<uint32_t> maxLexerErrors;
 
--- a/include/slang/parsing/Parser.h
+++ b/include/slang/parsing/Parser.h
@@ -135,6 +135,11 @@ struct SLANG_EXPORT ParserOptions {
 
     /// The version of the SystemVerilog language to use.
